  "output": "Repo B is self-contained: six modules, no imports, no other bindings of these names (grep for `^import|^from` returns nothing). So `pipeline/gate.py` is the whole story.\n\n## Control flow in `gate()` (file: `pipeline/gate.py`, function `gate`)\n\n```python\n1  def gate(mode):\n2      if mode == \"fast\":\n3          skip_tests(mode)\n4      else:\n5          run_tests(mode)\n6      publish(mode)\n```\n\nThere is no `return`, `raise`, or `break` anywhere in `gate()`, and the `if/else` on lines 2–5 has no `elif` and no inner nesting. The call graph is therefore: `skip_tests` XOR `run_tests` (mutually exclusive), then `publish` unconditionally on the merge point at line 6.\n\n**1. Can `skip_tests()` and `publish()` both execute in one call of `gate()`? — Yes.**\nConcrete path: `gate(\"fast\")` → line 2 true → line 3 calls `skip_tests(\"fast\")` → fall-through (no early exit) → line 6 calls `publish(\"fast\")`. The branch bodies of `skip_tests`/`run_tests` are just `return mode` (lines 10, 14), so they return normally and cannot raise, meaning `publish` at line 6 is in fact **always** reached. This matches the fixture's own documentation in `README.md:10`: `| gate.py | gate | publish runs after the branch merge, on every path |`.\n\n**2. Can `run_tests()` and `publish()` both execute in one call? — Yes.**\nConcrete path: `gate(\"slow\")` (or any `mode != \"fast\"`, including `None`) → line 2 false → line 5 calls `run_tests(mode)` → line 6 calls `publish(mode)`.\n\n**What is *not* possible:** `skip_tests()` and `run_tests()` together in one call — they sit in the two arms of the same `if/else` (lines 2–5). `publish()` is the only function with an unconditional call, so it accompanies exactly one of the two branch functions. It would be skipped only if `skip_tests`/`run_tests` raised, which their bodies (`return mode`) cannot do.\n\n(Note: I could not run `python -m provenlattice explain-symbol` — Bash was denied in this session — so the conclusion rests on direct source reading plus the repo-wide greps above.)\n\nANSWER: Yes to both — `gate(\"fast\")` executes `skip_tests` then `publish`; any other `mode` executes `run_tests` then `publish`. The two branch functions are mutually exclusive (`skip_tests` XOR `run_tests`), while `publish` at line 6 runs unconditionally on every path after the merge."
